{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-250994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${(1+\\frac{2b}{7})}^{9}=$$\\,1\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$b\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${b}^{2}+⋯$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996388/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d39e4ebb7a80d83f9880ffa1429b76076f21dd88c405b457781d201175b0c7"} {"question" "q-250994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=\\frac{2b}{7}$ and $n=9$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+\\frac{2b}{7})}^{9} & =1+9\\cdot \\frac{2b}{7}+\\frac{9(9-1)}{2!}\\cdot {(\\frac{2b}{7})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{18b}{7}+\\frac{9\\cdot 8}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4{b}^{2}}{49}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{18b}{7}+36\\cdot \\frac{4{b}^{2}}{49}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{18}{7}b+\\frac{144}{49}{b}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996388/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d39e4ebb7a80d83f9880ffa1429b76076f21dd88c405b457781d201175b0c7"} {"question" "q-250994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996388/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "15b5d744c87949518883e82d013a9ee012335dbd2cd03ee547125b1a9a71443f"} {"question" "q-250994" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996388/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d39e4ebb7a80d83f9880ffa1429b76076f21dd88c405b457781d201175b0c7"} {"question" "q-250994" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{18}{7}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996388/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d39e4ebb7a80d83f9880ffa1429b76076f21dd88c405b457781d201175b0c7"} {"question" "q-250994" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996388/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d39e4ebb7a80d83f9880ffa1429b76076f21dd88c405b457781d201175b0c7"} {"question" "q-250994" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{144}{49}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996388/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d39e4ebb7a80d83f9880ffa1429b76076f21dd88c405b457781d201175b0c7"} {"question" "q-12965" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$(1+a)^{9}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996388/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d39e4ebb7a80d83f9880ffa1429b76076f21dd88c405b457781d201175b0c7"} {"question" "q-12965" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The first three terms of the binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=a$ and $n=9$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+a)}^{9} & =1+9\\cdot a+\\frac{9(9-1)}{2!}\\cdot {a}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+9a+\\frac{9\\cdot 8}{2}\\cdot {a}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+9a+36{a}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996388/state.json" "file_sha256" "95d39e4ebb7a80d83f9880ffa1429b76076f21dd88c405b457781d201175b0c7"} {"question" "q-12965" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course